b.1.1 Company Overview (Why the Enterprise System Was Implemented) 
Company Name: Nestlé  
Nestlé, a global food and beverage company, faced challenges in managing its vast operations due to outdated and disconnected legacy systems. These inefficiencies caused difficulties in inventory tracking, supply chain management, and decision-making. To address these issues, Nestlé implemented SAP, an integrated ERP system, to unify its global operations, enhance logistics and supply chain efficiency, optimize production processes, and provide real-time access to critical business data. This transformation aimed to enable quicker market responses and improve overall operational effectiveness.  
b.2.2 Implementation Process 
Nestlé's SAP implementation followed a structured approach. First, the company conducted an internal evaluation to identify key inefficiencies in its existing systems, such as inventory management and order processing. After selecting SAP for its adaptability and scalability, Nestlé customized it to meet its operational needs. The company then migrated data from older systems and trained employees to ensure a smooth transition. Before the full deployment, rigorous testing was conducted to address any issues. Once the system went live, Nestlé closely monitored its performance and made necessary improvements to enhance functionality.  

b.e3.3 key Success Factors
Several factors contributed to the successful implementation of SAP at Nestlé. The company had a clear objective—to streamline operations and improve data-driven decision-making—which kept the project aligned with business goals. Strong project management ensured that each phase was executed effectively. Employee involvement through early training and testing helped minimize resistance to change. Nestlé also collaborated closely with SAP to customize the system and receive ongoing support. Additionally, change management strategies were effectively implemented to ensure employees adapted to the new system smoothly.  
b.4.Outcomes & Impact 
The integration of SAP at Nestlé significantly improved operations by connecting various departments, including sales, manufacturing, and supply chain. With real-time data access, decision-making became more efficient, leading to better inventory management, reduced waste, and enhanced supply chain agility. Automation reduced operational costs, allowing the company to reinvest in other business areas. The ability to track orders and shipments in real-time also resulted in improved customer service and satisfaction.   

Case Study 2: Failed Enterprise System Implementation*  

 Company Overview
c.1. company Name  Target Canada  
When Target expanded into Canada, the company aimed to implement SAP to manage its retail operations, including inventory, supply chain, and sales. The goal was to create a seamless, integrated system to handle logistics efficiently and ensure that stores were well-stocked. However, the rushed implementation of SAP and poor data management led to major disruptions.  
c.2. implementation Process & Challenges
Target Canada attempted to implement SAP rapidly to meet an aggressive launch timeline. The process involved selecting SAP for its robust features, customizing it to meet retail-specific needs, and migrating data from various sources. However, due to time constraints, thorough testing was not conducted, and data migration errors went unchecked. The system also lacked proper integration with supply chain partners, leading to inventory inaccuracies.  
 c.3. key Failure Factors
The failure of SAP implementation at Target Canada was caused by multiple factors. The system was rushed into operation before it was fully ready, and inadequate testing led to major functionality issues. Data migration errors resulted in incorrect inventory levels, causing stores to either overstock or run out of essential items. Additionally, the company underestimated the complexity of integrating SAP with its logistics network, leading to supply chain breakdowns.  
c.4.outcomes & Consequences 
The flawed implementation caused significant operational disruptions, with widespread inventory shortages and mismanagement. Customers frequently encountered empty shelves, while warehouses were overstocked with unnecessary products. These issues severely impacted sales and customer trust. The financial losses were substantial, ultimately contributing to Target Canada's decision to shut down its operations in Canada. The failure highlighted the importance of thorough system testing, realistic implementation timelines, and precise data migration.  
